  "answer": "An accused may be tried in his absence when it is proved that he has absconded and there is no immediate prospect of arresting him, and his attendance cannot be obtained without an unreasonable amount of delay, expense or inconvenience. In such a case the court may, in his absence, examine the prosecution witnesses, record their depositions and use those depositions as evidence at the inquiry or trial.",
